  (commentary generated by claude-fable-5)
Risk tone this morning is split down the middle: Nvidia's bullish sales outlook has reignited the AI trade, lifting Nasdaq futures 1.1% and the S&P 0.4%, while Europe lags — Euro Stoxx 50 off 0.4% and bank futures down 1.4% — as fiscal nerves bite. France has displaced Italy as the bond market's biggest European worry ahead of its budget, and the long end is paying for it: OATs, Bunds and Buxl all lower, steepening the curve. Treasuries are barely changed with jobless claims at their lowest since mid-May, keeping the labour market firm and attention squarely on Jackson Hole, where new Fed chair Warsh must convince markets — and reportedly a divided administration, with Bessent and Warsh at odds over who sets the price of money — that his inflation approach holds up.

The dollar is recouping losses into that event; BoE hike expectations meanwhile drift into 2027. Crude is steady, with Brent +0.4% near $88 as traders weigh rebuilding Hormuz flows from Kuwait and Qatar against Russian disruption risk with Ukraine talks at a dead end; prompt timespreads are softening into expiry and call skews easing, even as product cracks sit at multimonth highs after falling US stockpiles. TTF and Henry Hub are both up over 1%. The real action is in softs: arabica coffee is down 3.8% off record highs as Trump's withdrawn Colombia tariff threats unwind the scare premium, cocoa is up 4.4%, and OJ down 6%.

Metals are heavy across the board — copper, aluminium and gold all softer on the firmer dollar. Watch Jackson Hole and French fiscal headlines for the next rates catalyst.
